<!--
  Local rules for the SOC lab.

  Mounted read-only at /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml. The default
  ossec.conf ruleset already loads etc/rules, so no extra <rule_dir> is needed.

  100100-100199 is reserved here for threat-intel enrichment. Wazuh reserves
  everything below 100000 for its own ruleset, and the 100000+ range is the
  documented space for user rules — staying inside it means an upgrade that ships
  new built-in rules can never collide with these.
-->
<group name="threat_intel,misp,">

  <!--
    Parent rule. Matches the JSON events custom-misp.py writes back into
    analysisd, identified by the "integration": "misp" field it always sets.

    Level 0 deliberately: this rule exists to be inherited from, not to alert.
    Every real hit is classified by one of the children below, and a level 0
    parent keeps an unclassified hit out of the alert stream rather than
    producing a duplicate alongside its own child.
  -->
  <rule id="100100" level="0">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="integration">^misp$</field>
    <description>MISP threat-intel lookup result</description>
  </rule>

  <!--
    A hit on an indicator MISP flags for detection (to_ids=True) — meaning the
    indicator is considered reliable enough to alert on, not merely context.
    This is the alert an analyst should actually work.
  -->
  <rule id="100101" level="12">
    <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
    <field name="misp.to_ids">^True$</field>
    <description>MISP: known-bad $(misp.observable_kind) seen on $(misp.agent_name) — $(misp.attribute_type) matched threat intel [triggered by rule $(misp.source_rule_id)]</description>
    <group>threat_intel,misp_alert,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    A hit on an indicator MISP holds as context only (to_ids=False): worth
    recording and correlating, not worth waking anyone for. Splitting on to_ids
    rather than alerting identically on every hit is the difference between
    enrichment an analyst trusts and a second stream of noise.
  -->
  <rule id="100102" level="6">
    <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
    <field name="misp.to_ids">^False$</field>
    <description>MISP: $(misp.observable_kind) $(misp.observable) matched non-IDS threat intel on $(misp.agent_name)</description>
    <group>threat_intel,misp_context,</group>
  </rule>
</group>

<!--
  ============================================================================
  Phase 6 — custom detections, 100200-100299
  ============================================================================

  Each rule here exists because the stock ruleset does NOT already cover it.
  That constraint shaped the whole set: Wazuh already ships brute-force
  detection (5712/5763) and file-integrity alerts (550/553/554), so
  re-implementing those would add rule-count and no detection value. What the
  stock ruleset lacks is CONTEXT:

    - it detects a brute force, but not a brute force that then SUCCEEDED
    - it reports a file changed, but scores /etc/shadow the same as a scratch
      file, because it has no notion of which paths matter
    - it has no concept of the destination being known-malicious

  So these rules build ON the stock ones with <if_sid>/<if_matched_sid> rather
  than replacing them. A Wazuh upgrade that improves 5712 improves these too.

  Every rule carries a <mitre> block. That is not decoration: it is what lets an
  analyst pivot from an alert to the technique, and what makes coverage
  measurable against ATT&CK rather than asserted.
-->
<group name="soclab,custom_detections,">

  <!--
    D1 — Brute force that SUCCEEDED.

    The single highest-value detection in this set. A brute force on its own is
    noise: the internet knocks on every SSH port constantly, and stock rule 5712
    fires on it all day. A brute force followed by a successful login FROM THE
    SAME SOURCE is a credential compromise in progress, and nothing in the stock
    ruleset joins those two facts.

    Level 14, above the brute force itself (10) and far above a successful login
    (3), because neither of those parts is interesting alone — the conjunction
    is the whole signal.

    T1110.001 for the guessing, T1078 because what the attacker now holds is a
    valid account, which is the thing that actually persists.
  -->
  <rule id="100200" level="14" timeframe="600">
    <if_sid>5715</if_sid>
    <if_matched_sid>5712</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>Successful SSH login from $(srcip), which was brute-forcing moments ago — probable credential compromise</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110.001</id>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_success,attack,soclab_d1,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    D2 — Modification of a credential store or persistence location.

    Stock 550/553/554 report that a file changed, at a fixed severity, with no
    idea which file it was. A checksum change on /etc/shadow and one on a
    scratch file in /var/lab are the same alert. This rule re-scores the small
    set of paths where a change is nearly always either an attacker establishing
    persistence or an attacker taking credentials.

    The path list is deliberately short. Every path added here costs an analyst
    an alert, so the bar is "a change here is suspicious by default", not "this
    file is important".

    T1098.004 covers authorized_keys (SSH key persistence); T1053.003 covers
    cron; T1136 covers account creation via passwd/shadow edits.
  -->
  <rule id="100210" level="12">
    <if_sid>550,553,554</if_sid>
    <!-- The field is `file`, NOT `syscheck.path`.
         The alert JSON nests the path at syscheck.path, so that is the obvious
         name to match on — and it silently matches nothing. analysisd reports no
         error: the rule loads, is evaluated, and never fires, so the only symptom
         is a detection that quietly does not exist. Wazuh's own ruleset is no
         help here, because no stock rule does path-based FIM matching at all.
         Confirmed empirically by firing both candidates at one probe file. -->
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">/etc/(passwd|shadow|gshadow|sudoers)|/etc/sudoers\.d/|/\.ssh/authorized_keys|/etc/cron|/var/spool/cron</field>
    <!-- No $(agent.name) in any description here: $(...) expansion reaches only
         DECODED fields, so it renders as an empty string. The alert JSON already
         carries the agent at top level, so naming the host in the text is
         redundant anyway — but the empty gap looks like a bug to whoever reads
         the alert, which is worse than omitting it. -->
    <description>Security-critical file created or modified: $(file)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1098.004</id>
      <id>T1053.003</id>
      <id>T1136</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>syscheck,attack,soclab_d2,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    D3 — New executable appearing in a system binary directory.

    Distinct from D2 on purpose. D2 is about MODIFYING something that already
    exists; this is about something NEW appearing where only the package manager
    should be writing. On a container that installs nothing at runtime, a new
    file in /usr/bin is close to definitionally unexpected.

    Scoped to rule 554 (file ADDED) rather than 550 (changed), because a package
    update legitimately changes files in these directories all the time, and
    alerting on that would train the analyst to ignore the rule.

    T1036.005 — a binary placed in a trusted system location is the classic way
    to make malware look legitimate.
  -->
  <rule id="100220" level="12">
    <if_sid>554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">^/(usr/)?(local/)?s?bin/</field>
    <description>New executable dropped into a system binary directory: $(file)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1036.005</id>
      <id>T1543</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>syscheck,attack,soclab_d3,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    D4a — Outbound connection observed (feeder rule, intentionally quiet).

    Level 3: this is an OBSERVATION, not a finding. Its job is to exist so that
    the MISP integration sees the event at all — integratord only receives
    alerts, and stock firewall rule 4100 is level 0, which never alerts. Without
    this rule, outbound connections would be invisible to enrichment.

    Splitting "observe" from "judge" is deliberate. The alternative — one rule
    that both notices the connection and decides it is bad — would either be too
    noisy (every outbound connection is an alert) or unable to enrich at all.
  -->
  <!-- Internal chatter: matched first, scored 0, and therefore never alerted.

       This inverted shape is not stylistic. Expressing "destination is NOT
       internal" directly turned out to be impossible with the primitives that
       actually work in this version, and each alternative failed differently:

         * <field name="dstip"> is rejected outright — srcip/dstip/srcport/
           dstport/protocol/action/srcuser/dstuser are STATIC fields with their
           own rule elements, and <field> addresses only dynamic ones. It does
           not merely fail: analysisd logs "Field 'dstip' is static" and refuses
           to load THE ENTIRE FILE, silently taking every other rule with it.

         * <dstip>!CIDR</dstip> parses but never matches anything. Verified with
           both !172.16.0.0/12 and an exact !172.20.0.5 against an unrelated
           destination. Positive values and positive CIDRs match correctly, so
           the address is being read; the negation is what is broken.

         * A CDB list with not_address_match_key is the documented answer, but a
           list is inert until compiled to .cdb, and nothing in this deployment
           compiles it — not an analysisd restart, and not the API upload, which
           reports success and produces no .cdb. analysisd then warns "List ...
           could not be loaded. Rule '100230' will be ignored" and carries on.

         * Even a positive LIST is rejected: <dstip> accepts exactly one address
           or one CIDR. Comma-separated and pipe-separated lists both fail with
           "Invalid ip address", which — like the static-field case — takes the
           whole file down rather than skipping the rule.

       What does work is one single-CIDR rule per range, scored 0, ordered ahead
       of 100230, relying on Wazuh's first-match-wins evaluation: an internal
       destination matches one of these and stops, so 100230 below only ever sees
       genuinely external traffic. Verbose, but built only from primitives that
       were verified to work rather than from documented ones that do not. -->
  <rule id="100227" level="0">
    <if_sid>4100</if_sid>
    <dstip>10.0.0.0/8</dstip>
    <description>Internal connection (10/8) — not outbound, ignored</description>
    <group>firewall,soclab_d4,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100228" level="0">
    <if_sid>4100</if_sid>
    <dstip>172.16.0.0/12</dstip>
    <description>Internal connection (172.16/12, Docker bridges) — not outbound, ignored</description>
    <group>firewall,soclab_d4,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100229" level="0">
    <if_sid>4100</if_sid>
    <dstip>192.168.0.0/16</dstip>
    <description>Internal connection (192.168/16) — not outbound, ignored</description>
    <group>firewall,soclab_d4,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100230" level="3">
    <if_sid>4100</if_sid>
    <description>Outbound connection from $(srcip) to external host $(dstip):$(dstport)</description>
    <group>firewall,soclab_d4,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    D4b — Outbound connection to a destination MISP knows.

    The judgement half of D4. Fires on the MISP lookup result (parent 100100)
    when the lookup was triggered by a firewall event, which is what makes this
    "we called out to known-malicious infrastructure" rather than the generic
    "we saw a known-bad IP somewhere" that rule 100101 already covers.

    Matching on the triggering rule's GROUPS rather than its ID is deliberate:
    group membership is stable across Wazuh upgrades, stock rule IDs are not
    guaranteed to be.

    T1071.001 — outbound C2 over an application-layer protocol. This is the
    direction that matters: inbound scanning is background noise, but a host
    reaching OUT to known C2 means the host is already compromised.
  -->
  <rule id="100231" level="13">
    <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
    <field name="misp.source_rule_groups" type="pcre2">firewall</field>
    <field name="misp.observable_kind">^ip$</field>
    <description>Outbound connection to known-malicious infrastructure from $(misp.agent_name): $(misp.observable) — MISP $(misp.attribute_type)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
      <id>T1571</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>threat_intel,misp_alert,attack,soclab_d4,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    D5 — Authentication attack from a source already known to threat intel.

    Rule 5712 says "someone is brute-forcing". Rule 100101 says "we saw a
    known-bad IP". This says both at once, which is a materially different
    triage decision: an unknown source brute-forcing is routine internet noise,
    while a source that a threat feed already lists as hostile infrastructure is
    a targeted, attributable attempt worth acting on immediately.

    Same groups-not-IDs reasoning as 100231.
  -->
  <rule id="100240" level="13">
    <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
    <field name="misp.source_rule_groups" type="pcre2">authentication_fail</field>
    <field name="misp.observable_kind">^ip$</field>
    <description>Authentication attack from threat-intel-listed host $(misp.observable) against $(misp.agent_name)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>threat_intel,misp_alert,authentication_failures,attack,soclab_d5,</group>
  </rule>



</group>

<!--
  ============================================================================
  Cross-signal correlation — deliberately LAST in this file
  ============================================================================

  Rule order is load-bearing here, and finding that out cost most of Phase 7.

  <if_matched_sid> and <if_matched_group> are resolved when the file is PARSED,
  against the rules seen SO FAR. This rule used to sit near the top, next to the
  enrichment rules it correlates, and pointed forward at rules defined hundreds
  of lines below it. analysisd resolves that reference to nothing and drops the
  rule:

      WARNING: (7620): Signature ID '100240' was not found.
               Invalid 'if_matched_sid'. Rule '100103' will be ignored.

  A WARNING, at manager startup, in a log nobody reads twice — and then a rule
  that is absent from the running ruleset while still being present, and
  correct-looking, in the file. Same failure shape as the uncompiled CDB list in
  Phase 6: the ruleset loads, the file reviews clean, the detection does not
  exist. Correlation rules therefore go at the BOTTOM, after everything they
  count.
-->
<group name="threat_intel,misp,">

  <!--
    Repeated threat-intel matches from one agent, across DIFFERENT signals.

    One intel hit is an observation. Four inside five minutes — a brute force
    from a listed host, then that host's implant beaconing to listed C2 — is the
    shape of a host that is already owned, and it is the single alert that names
    the incident rather than one of its stages. Level 14, above every individual
    detection it summarises.

    Counting the misp_alert GROUP rather than a specific rule id is what keeps
    this alive. Its original form counted rule 100101, and Phase 6 silently
    killed it: D5 (100240) is a more specific sibling, so every failed login from
    a listed host began matching D5 INSTEAD of 100101 and the counter this rule
    watches stopped advancing. A group that every intel detection joins survives
    the next sibling somebody writes.
  -->
  <rule id="100103" level="14" frequency="4" timeframe="300">
    <if_matched_group>misp_alert</if_matched_group>
    <!-- Correlate on the agent, via the decoded field the integration adds.
         NOT same_source_ip: these events are injected by the integration and
         carry no srcip of their own, so an srcip-based correlation would sit
         there never firing. -->
    <same_field>misp.agent_name</same_field>
    <description>MISP: repeated threat-intel matches from $(misp.agent_name) — possible active compromise</description>
    <!-- Deliberately NOT in misp_alert: this rule counts that group, and a rule
         that belongs to the group it counts would feed its own counter. -->
    <group>threat_intel,misp_correlation,attack,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
